            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 9th Grade (CEFR: A2.4)</td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> Transportation</td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> The Benefits of Public Transportation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td colspan="3" style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td colspan="3" style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td colspan="3" style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Comprehend discussions on familiar subjects. Interactive: Contribute to instructions in workshops.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can comprehend discussions on road safety rules (e.g., "Identify why wearing a seatbelt is life-saving." Using crosswalks in busy areas, like Avenida Balboa, is important to prevent an accident." Understand why using the sidewalk or staying out of the road is essential when walking near unlit roads in rural areas.")
Can follow and contribute to instructions in workshops about public transportation (e.g., "You should walk to the bus stop, then take bus number ten to get to the shopping mall.")
Can follow instructions for a group activity on transportation options (e.g., "I think using the bus is more cost-effective than taking a taxi because it's cheaper.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

## SECTION 3 — AOA STAGES TABLE
<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage aims to engage students, activate prior knowledge about transportation, and introduce core vocabulary and pronunciation in preparation for listening tasks.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a collage of images showing various forms of transportation in Panama City (e.g., Metrobus, Metro, taxi, private car, ferry, bike, walking). Students are asked to identify them and briefly state how they get to school or other common destinations. Teacher prompts: "What transportation do you *prefer*?" "What are the *benefits* of taking the Metro?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces 8-10 key vocabulary words relevant to the theme "The Benefits of Public Transportation." Words like *bus, train, passenger, fare, commute, convenient, efficient, safety, traffic, sidewalk, seatbelt, rush hour*. The teacher models the pronunciation, focusing on stress in multisyllabic words (e.g., "transportation," "convenience," "passenger," "schedule") and distinguishing between long and short vowels (e.g., /æ/ in "passenger" vs /ɑː/ in "port"). Sentences like "The *passenger* must *tap in* their card on the *bus*" are used for context.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding using questions and examples.
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is a *bus* a *private* or *public* vehicle?" (Expected answer: Public)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If a bus is *efficient*, does it take a lot of time or less time?" (Expected answer: Less time)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is a *fare*? Is it a ticket or money for the trip?" (Expected answer: Money for the trip)</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage provides authentic listening input related to the scenario "Transportation" and introduces the theme's core ideas, followed by general comprehension tasks.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content:</b> Students will listen to an audio recording. The recording is a short dialogue between two Panamanian teenagers discussing their daily *commute* and the *benefits* of using public transportation, specifically the Metrobus and Metro. It will include mentions of *safety rules* (e.g., "You *have to* wear a *seatbelt* on the bus.") and comparisons (e.g., "The Metro is *faster* than the *bus* in *rush hour*."). The dialogue also contains phrasal verbs like "get on" and "get off".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Audio recording (approx. 1.5-2 minutes) of a dialogue or a short public service announcement (PSA) about road safety and public transport convenience. The dialogue will be presented clearly and at an A2.4 level, incorporating the target grammar and vocabulary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening: Receptive: Comprehend discussions on familiar subjects.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students listen to the audio twice.</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> "What are the two friends mainly talking about?" (Expected: How to travel in the city / Public transportation).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Statements:</b> Students listen and mark statements as true or false. E.g., "The girl thinks taking the bus is always faster than the Metro." (False). "They agree that wearing a seatbelt is important." (True).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match phrases related to benefits with the correct mode of transport mentioned. E.g., "More *convenient* than driving" -> Metro, "*Cheaper* than a taxi" -> Bus.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage focuses on developing accuracy in listening comprehension, specifically targeting the new vocabulary, grammar structures, and pronunciation. Students engage in controlled and semi-controlled activities.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice identifying and comprehending specific vocabulary (e.g., *fare, schedule, delay, rush hour, seatbelt, sidewalk*), modal verbs for advice and obligation (*should, have to*), comparatives (*faster, cheaper*), and phrasal verbs (*get on, get off*). Pronunciation practice focuses on word stress and vowel contrasts from the modeled words.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Gap-fill Listening:</b> The teacher provides a transcript of the audio with key vocabulary words, modal verbs, and comparatives omitted. Students listen to the audio a third time and fill in the blanks. E.g., "You ___ always wear a ___ when you travel by car." or "The train is ___ than the bus."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Sentence Completion (Listening for Choice):</b> The teacher plays specific sentences or short segments from the audio again. For each segment, students are given two word options (e.g., *passenger/driver*, *should/have to*, *faster/slower*) and must listen carefully to choose the correct one. E.g., "The speaker says you [should / shouldn't] use crosswalks in busy areas." "They mention the bus [gets on / gets off] at the next stop."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction / Peer Check:</b> Students work in pairs to compare their answers for both the gap-fill and sentence completion activities. They re-listen to challenging parts of the audio together, explaining their choices and correcting any discrepancies. The teacher circulates, offering support and clarifying pronunciation as needed, particularly for word stress (e.g., "transportation" vs. "transport") and vowel sounds (e.g., /æ/ in "tap" vs. /eɪ/ in "tape").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">12-15 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">In this stage, students apply their listening comprehension and new language in a communicative task, focusing on fluency and interaction within a realistic scenario.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task: "Public Transportation Workshop – Giving Advice"</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in small groups (3-4 students). The teacher provides each group with 3-4 short audio clips of different scenarios or problems related to transportation in Panama. Each scenario describes a situation requiring advice or instructions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario Examples:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Audio Clip 1: "A tourist wants to get from Albrook Bus Terminal to Casco Viejo. They want the *cheapest* and most *convenient* way."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Audio Clip 2: "A parent is worried about their child walking home from school near a busy *highway* with no *sidewalk*."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Audio Clip 3: "Someone is complaining about the heavy *traffic* during *rush hour* when they *commute* by car every day."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Each group listens to a scenario, discusses it, and collaboratively formulates advice or instructions, using modal verbs (*should, have to*), comparatives (*faster, safer, cheaper*), and target vocabulary (e.g., *bus, train, safety, convenience, traffic, seatbelt, sidewalk, crosswalks*). They *contribute to instructions* in their "workshop." One student from each group presents their collective advice to the class.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Emphasis is on listening comprehension of the scenario, collaborative problem-solving, and the communicative use of target language to give practical, safety-focused, and convenient advice.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage involves formative assessment to check students' listening comprehension and their ability to use the target language effectively, followed by student self-reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Public Transportation Workshop" activity. The teacher will circulate and note down instances of target vocabulary use, grammar structures, and active listening skills demonstrated by students.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students participate actively in their groups during the workshop, listening to the audio scenarios, discussing solutions, and verbally contributing their advice/instructions, demonstrating comprehension of safety rules and transportation options.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of target vocabulary (e.g., "transportation," "convenience," "passenger," "schedule").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of modal verbs (*should, have to*) for advice/obligation and comparatives (*faster, cheaper, safer*).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and relevance of advice/instructions in response to the listened scenarios.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Active listening and collaborative contribution to group discussions.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a short exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was one new word I learned today about public transportation or safety?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What is one benefit of public transportation that I heard about today?</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This final stage encourages both student and teacher to reflect on the learning process, identify areas for improvement, and establish a clear connection to the next lesson in the sequence.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How well did I understand the different discussions and instructions about public transportation today? (e.g., "I understood most of the advice.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What did I find challenging about listening to different transportation options or safety rules? What will I try to do better next time?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What aspects of the lesson worked particularly well for introducing new vocabulary and practicing listening comprehension at an A2.4 level? (e.g., The audio dialogue was engaging, the scenario-based task promoted good interaction).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for students who struggled to comprehend specific details or pronounce multisyllabic words? (e.g., More targeted pronunciation drills, pre-teaching more vocabulary, providing visuals for complex phrases).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did the lesson effectively address the specific objective and learning outcome for listening skills?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> "In Lesson 2, students will transition from listening to reading. They will read a short informational text or article about 'The Benefits of Public Transportation' and road safety rules, utilizing the vocabulary and concepts introduced in this listening lesson to deepen their comprehension and contextual understanding."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>